\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420111} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f2c3dd-df0a-57f1-9b19-b251ffe9a72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420122} {:answer/id #uuid "bc26c2f9-39d9-5339-a3e8-be5d2e945ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420123} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d13b79-c01f-5ddb-888f-f826b5a1451c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420124} {:answer/id #uuid "e83e0132-215d-5133-98be-6554e8873e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e83e0132-215d-5133-98be-6554e8873e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420128} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0a832f-24dd-571f-b252-8ec9cfdfbd85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12feee0e-318a-5930-8eca-dd111ec7b1c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109091" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=1,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $b=\\frac{1}{8}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}X] & ={(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=1.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+9\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}X] & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
